    "q-129982": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129982",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼B(6,0.6),$ compute $P(X\\ge 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,4,5,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X<2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X<2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.6)^{0}(0.4)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.6)^{1}(0.4)^{6-1} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{0}(0.4)^{6}+6(0.6)^{1}(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.0410\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0410 \\\\  & =0.9590\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
